<div class="tldr"><p><strong>Short answer:</strong> South America's ski season runs from about June to October, and July and August usually bring the best snow. In Chile, Valle Nevado, La Parva, and El Colorado (together called Tres Valles) sit an hour or two above Santiago, and Portillo is a classic on the road to Argentina. Southern Chile has volcano skiing at Nevados de Chillán and Corralco. In Argentina, Cerro Catedral near Bariloche, Las Leñas, Chapelco, and Cerro Castor near Ushuaia are the main draws. Valle Nevado is on the Ikon Pass, and Corralco is on the Indy Pass.</p></div>

<h2>When to ski in South America</h2>
<p>The Andes' ski season is the mirror image of North America's.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>June:</strong> resorts open as storms arrive. Early coverage depends on the year.</li>
  <li><strong>July:</strong> peak season, with good snow and local winter school holidays, so expect crowds.</li>
  <li><strong>August:</strong> often the best combination of snow depth and fewer crowds.</li>
  <li><strong>September and October:</strong> spring conditions and sunny days, with some resorts closing earlier than others.</li>
</ul>
<p>Snow in the Andes tends to come in big storms followed by long stretches of sun, so timing matters. Check each resort's pattern on pages like <%= link_to "best time to ski Portillo", best_time_resort_path("portillo") %> and <%= link_to "best time to ski Cerro Catedral", best_time_resort_path("cerro-catedral") %>.</p>

<h2>Chile: resorts near Santiago</h2>
<ul>
  <li><%= link_to "Valle Nevado", resort_path("valle-nevado") %>: the largest of the Tres Valles resorts, with high-altitude terrain and views across the Andes.</li>
  <li><%= link_to "La Parva", resort_path("la-parva") %>: a high resort with a mix of groomers and open bowls, linked with its neighbors.</li>
  <li><%= link_to "El Colorado", resort_path("el-colorado") %>: the closest of the three to Santiago, popular with day skiers.</li>
  <li><%= link_to "Portillo", resort_path("portillo") %>: a historic resort near the Argentine border, known for its lakeside hotel and steep terrain. National ski teams often train here during the northern summer.</li>
</ul>
<p>The roads up from Santiago are winding mountain roads, and authorities can require chains or restrict traffic during storms. Many visitors use shuttles or transfers rather than driving. These resorts are also high, so take the first day easy, drink water, and watch for signs of altitude sickness.</p>

<h2>Chile: volcano skiing in the south</h2>
<ul>
  <li><%= link_to "Nevados de Chillán", resort_path("nevados-de-chillan") %>: on the flanks of an active volcano complex, with tree skiing and natural hot springs nearby. The nearest airport is Concepción (CCP).</li>
  <li><%= link_to "Corralco", resort_path("corralco") %>: on Lonquimay volcano, surrounded by araucaria (monkey puzzle) forests, reached through Temuco (ZCO). It's on the Indy Pass.</li>
</ul>
<p>Volcano slopes and the terrain beyond resort boundaries are uncontrolled avalanche terrain. If you go beyond the boundary, carry rescue gear and hire a local guide. See <%= link_to "avalanche safety gear explained", guide_path("avalanche-safety-gear") %>.</p>

<h2>Argentina</h2>
<ul>
  <li><%= link_to "Cerro Catedral", resort_path("cerro-catedral") %>: South America's largest ski area by many measures, above the lake town of Bariloche (BRC), with lively nightlife.</li>
  <li><%= link_to "Las Leñas", resort_path("las-lenas") %>: known for steep, high terrain, reached via Mendoza (MDZ) and a long transfer.</li>
  <li><%= link_to "Chapelco", resort_path("chapelco") %>: tree-lined runs above San Martín de los Andes, with its own nearby airport (CPC).</li>
  <li><%= link_to "Cerro Castor", resort_path("cerro-castor") %>: near Ushuaia (USH) at the southern tip of the continent, with a long season thanks to its far-south location.</li>
</ul>

