<h2>Passes</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Ikon Pass:</strong> Valle Nevado, with 7 days on the Ikon Pass and 5 on the Ikon Base Pass. See <%= link_to "Ikon Pass resorts", ikon_pass_resorts_path %>.</li>
  <li><strong>Mountain Collective:</strong> Valle Nevado, with 2 days.</li>
  <li><strong>Indy Pass:</strong> Corralco. See <%= link_to "Indy Pass resorts", indy_pass_resorts_path %>.</li>
  <li><strong>Others:</strong> buy lift tickets directly from each resort.</li>
</ul>
<p>Your North American season pass usually renews before South America's season begins, so check which pass year covers your dates before you book.</p>

<h2>Who a South America ski trip suits</h2>
<p>A trip to the Andes makes sense if you want to ski during the northern summer, keep your legs going between seasons, or see a very different kind of mountain. North American and European racers and freestyle teams often hold training camps here in August and September for the same reason. The terrain is big and open, with few trees at the high Chilean resorts, so it rewards confident intermediate and advanced skiers. Beginners can still have a great time on groomed runs, especially at Cerro Catedral, El Colorado, and Valle Nevado, where ski schools are well established. If you want trees and powder in a forest, look south to Chapelco, Nevados de Chillán, and Corralco.</p>
<p>Spanish is the main language, though resort staff at the bigger areas are used to international visitors. Plan for long transfer days between regions, and keep a weather day in your schedule because storms can close roads and lifts for a day or two.</p>

<h2>Planning tips</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Base yourself well:</strong> Santiago works for day trips to Tres Valles, but staying on the mountain saves time on the road. Bariloche is a town base with buses to Cerro Catedral.</li>
  <li><strong>Combine countries:</strong> Chile and Argentina are close, but crossing the Andes by road depends on weather and border hours, so flying between regions is often more reliable.</li>
  <li><strong>Pack for big swings:</strong> strong high-altitude sun, cold storms, and warm spring afternoons can all happen in one trip. Bring sunscreen and goggles for both bright and flat light. See the <%= link_to "ski trip packing list", guide_path("ski-trip-packing-list") %>.</li>
</ul>
<p>See every resort ranked by this week's forecast on the <%= link_to "South America snow report", region_path("south-america") %>, or by country on the <%= link_to "Chile", region_path("chile") %> and <%= link_to "Argentina", region_path("argentina") %> pages. For Australia and New Zealand too, see the <%= link_to "Southern Hemisphere snow report", region_path("southern-hemisphere") %>.</p>

